feat(api): name the four lifecycle states and show their evidence (#256)

Three words could not tell a run that is quiet from one that is over, and the
word they collapsed into read as a claim that an agent had died. Four states,
each derived by query from recorded facts and their order — no state column, no
state table, no event, field, enum or source changed:

  active     not ended; never withdrawn, or activity newer than the newest
             withdrawal
  lapsed     the newest fact is a withdrawal, inside the restore horizon
  abandoned  the newest fact is a withdrawal, past the horizon
  retired    ended by its harness or a human

Every answer carries the evidence behind it: last activity, withdrawal instant,
restorable-until, parent run, and the horizon the answer was computed with.
Absent facts are omitted rather than sent as Go's zero time. The overview gains
lapsed and abandoned counts; neither is counted as active.

"Abandoned" now reads "nothing heard since X; the identity can no longer be
restored" — a statement about what this system will no longer do, never about
what became of the agent. "Expired" survives only as the run_expired event's own
name in the timeline.

Driven by FE-129 (the four states), FE-130 (the evidence line), FE-131 (the
overview counts) and FE-132 (no rendered string asserts an agent is dead), all
four observed failing first; API-030 to API-033 cover the derivations, the
evidence members and the horizon's own arithmetic.

The public verification page is unchanged.

